WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.080
Mind Your Decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.040
At a rectangular table are four mathematicians A, B, C and D.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.540
They sit at the following distances to the corners of the table.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.440
A salt shaker S is placed on the table.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:25.460
Consider the distance from each person to the saltshaker and take the sum of these distances.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.200
At some point, the sum of these distances will be a minimum value.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:37.340
The question is: What is the minimum value of SA + SB + SC + SD?

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.780
This problem has tremendous practical applications.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:48.060
You could imagine the four points are either towns or railway stations and you want to calculate

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:54.540
the least amount of roads or tracks that you would need to connect these towns or railway stations.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:01.580
You could also imagine the four points are friends and they want to calculate the most environmentally friendly point to meet,

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.000
that is the one that minimizes the sum of the distances they have to travel.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.700
You can really use this problem for many applications in teamwork.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.360
I adapted this problem from a Sri Lanka Olympiad and I thank Chanul for the suggestion.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:23.840
Pause the video if you'd like to give this problem a try, and when you're ready, keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.340
Imagine we have four points in the plane that form a convex quadrilateral.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:48.920
We want to determine the point S such that the sum of distances to these four points is a minimum value.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:56.400
We will prove the rather fascinating result: S will be at the intersection of AC and BD.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.580
First let's prove why this is true.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.280
Consider the segment AC

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:10.560
For any point X in the plane, we can connect AX and XC. Let's consider those distances.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.480
Well, by the triangle inequality AC will be less than or equal to AX + CX.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.200
Then, AC is equal to AS + CS.

00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:29.429
We will put this inequality to the side and for the same point X, we will consider the line segment BD.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.180
We will do a very similar sort of argument.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:39.360
By the triangle inequality, BD is less than or equal to BX + DX.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.800
BD is equal to BS + DS. We thus have another inequality.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.680
Let's put these two inequalities together by adding them. What happens?

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:57.180
Well on the left hand side, we get the sum of the four distances from each of these points to S,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:02.360
and on the right hand side, we have the sum of distances from some other point X.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:08.600
And we have shown that S is a minimum value; no other point can be less than that.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.680
Therefore, this is a minimal value.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:15.040
Let's use this result to solve the problem.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:20.360
We will first determine where S is by connecting AC and BD.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.900
Their intersection point will be S.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.760
From here, we need to calculate AS + SC + BS + SD.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.400
We'll do that by using right triangles.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.460
First, let's calculate the length of AC.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:41.880
We have one right triangle here. One leg will be equal to 22 and the other leg will be equal to 60.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.300
Therefore we can calculate the length of the hypotenuse using the Gougu theorem

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.840
and we get 2 times the square root of 1021.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:52.740
This will be equal to AS + CS.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:58.120
We can then do the same sort of calculation to determine the length of BD.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.560
We have a right triangle where one leg is 25 and the other leg is 40.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.920
We can therefore calculate the length of BD using the Gougu theorem

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.560
and we get 5 times the square root of 89.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.500
This will be equal to BS + TS.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:18.900
We then can calculate the sum of the four distances and that will be equal to

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:24.240
2 times the square root of 1021, plus 5 times the square root of 89

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:29.060
and that's approximately 111.08 and that's our answer.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:35.700
It's a rather fascinating optimization problem with an elegant geometric solution.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.980
Thanks for making Mind Your Decisions one of the best channels on YouTube.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:44.040
As always, thanks for watching and thanks for your support.

